{"id":"de136ad3-2f39-4318-8eff-4541839b90c1","object":"chat.completion","created":1785004436,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    # Validate each segment\\n    for s in segments:\\n        if not all(k in s for k in ('name', 'importance', 'minimum', 'capacity')):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required field in segment\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(s['name'], str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Segment name must be string\\\")\\n        if not all(isinstance(s[k], int) for k in ('importance', 'minimum', 'capacity')):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Importance, minimum, capacity must be integers\\\")\\n        if s['minimum'] < 0 or s['capacity'] < 0 or s['importance'] < 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Values must be non-negative\\\")\\n    # Sum minimums and capacities\\n    total_min = sum(s['minimum'] for s in segments)\\n    total_cap = sum(s['capacity'] for s in segments)\\n    if budget < total_min:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Budget below total minimum quotas\\\")\\n    if budget > total_cap:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Budget exceeds total capacity\\\")\\n    # Initialize allocations at minimum\\n    allocations = [s['minimum'] for s in segments]\\n    allocated = total_min\\n    # While budget remains\\n    while allocated < budget:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_score = -1.0\\n        for i, s in enumerate(segments):\\n            if allocations[i] >= s['capacity']:\\n                continue\\n            next_pos = allocations[i] + 1\\n            score = s['importance'] / next_pos\\n            # Better score, or tie and earlier index\\n            if score > best_score:\\n                best_score = score\\n                best_idx = i\\n            elif score == best_score and best_idx == -1:\\n                best_idx = i  # first encountered (since we iterate in order, ties go to earlier index)\\n            # Note: if score == best_score and best_idx already set, we keep earlier index because we only update on >; ties implicitly keep earlier due to iteration order and condition.\\n        if best_idx == -1:\\n            # Should not happen because budget <= total_cap and there should be room somewhere\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"No available capacity to allocate remaining budget\\\")\\n        allocations[best_idx] += 1\\n        allocated += 1\\n    # Build by_name dict\\n    by_name = {}\\n    for i, s in enumerate(segments):\\n        by_name[s['name']] = allocations[i]\\n    # Total is budget (should equal sum(allocations))\\n    total = sum(allocations)\\n    return {\\n        'allocations': allocations,\\n        'by_name': by_name,\\n        'total': total\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements the described algorithm. The case has segments list with name, importance, minimum, capacity, and a budget. The output must have allocations (list of integers same order as segments? Probably yes: allocations per segment in the same order as segments appear in case.segments), by_name (dict mapping segment name to allocated tokens), total (sum of allocations). The algorithm: start each segment at its minimum. Sum minimums: if budget < sum_minimums, raise ValueError. Also if budget > total_capacity (sum of capacities), raise ValueError? The spec says \"Reject budgets below the quota sum or above total capacity.\" So we need to check both. Then while budget > allocated so far, we give one token to the segment maximizing importance / (next allocated position). \"next allocated position\" is the number of tokens already allocated plus one? The formula: importance divided by its next allocated position. That is typical for priority-based allocation like \"proportional allocation\" but here it's greedy: for each token, compute score = importance / (current_alloc + 1). Pick max, break ties by original order. Then increment that segment's allocation, reduce budget by 1. Continue until budget exhausted. Need to ensure we don't exceed capacity per segment: before allocating, check if segment is at capacity; if so, skip. Actually the algorithm must enforce capacity: we must not allocate beyond capacity. So when picking the best segment, we should consider only those not at capacity. Also the budget may be less than sum of capacities, but we must exactly allocate budget. So we need to loop until allocated_total == budget. Return allocations list (same order as segments), by_name dict, total = budget. Ensure no mutation of case. Use only standard library.\n\nWe'll implement as described. Output must be JSON object with \"implementation\" key containing source code string. Need to escape newlines etc.\n\nWe'll write the code carefully.\n\nAlso note: The output schema says allocations items type integer, by_name values integer, total integer. So fine.\n\nEdge cases: tie-breaking by original order (index in segments list). Use enumerate.\n\nWe'll handle ValueError.\n\nWe'll also validate that each segment has name, importance, minimum, capacity as integers >=0? Not needed beyond validation.\n\nImplementation: def solve(case): segments = case['segments']; budget = case['budget']; etc.\n\nReturn dict.\n\nNow produce JSON response with one key \"implementation\"."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1496,"completion_tokens":1158,"total_tokens":2654,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":513},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":88},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}